From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SacredTrees <121399723+SacredTrees@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 21:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] WebCore: page and worker scripts translated once

On a 68040 compiling a page's scripts (parsing, bytecode) is a large part
of a page's time: 0.85 s of bbc.co.uk's, 3.2 s of reddit's, every visit.
JavaScriptCore already asks a script's SourceProvider for bytecode it saved
before (cachedBytecode), hands it the script's when it makes them
(cacheBytecode) and each function's when that function is first called
(updateCache), and tells it to write (commitCachedBytecode); WebCore's
providers kept nothing.

AmigaScriptBytecode (bindings/js/amiga) does it for both of WebCore's
providers, CachedScriptSourceProvider (scripts loaded for a page, module
scripts) and ScriptBufferSourceProvider (workers', importScripts and the
rest), as javascript.library's own cache does (gfopenbrowser's
src/jslib/jsl_cache.cpp): one file per script text in the folder the
browser names (setAmigaScriptBytecodeFolder; none, the default: nothing
kept), named by an FNV-1a hash and the length of the text, read when the
script is first compiled, written when the provider goes or the code cache
is written. A file another task changed meanwhile is left alone, and a
write that fails takes the file away.

A script is kept only the second time its text runs (the names seen once
are in the folder's "seen" file), so a page visited once costs no writes.
What is to be written waits in a list; the browser writes one file at a
time when its page is idle (writeAmigaScriptBytecode()). Each file is
made in memory and written with one write: putting each function's code
in place with a seek and a short write each took 10 s for bbc.co.uk's
8 MB on AmigaDOS. Scripts under 1 KB are not kept.
JavaScriptCore checks the text's hash, its length and the engine's version
again when it decodes, so a stale or foreign file is only made afresh.

The browser writes the code cache when a page has loaded: CodeCache.h needs
the parser's headers, which are not installed, so JavaScriptCore gives it
JSC::amigaWriteCodeCache(VM&) (runtime/AmigaCodeCacheWrite.h, a private
header on the Amiga), which calls CodeCache::write().

